From 4f7a4ad528df9532ac6cc84546add991764ac381 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Lamb <paul@paulscode.com>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 06:02:19 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] electrum: protocol 1.8 on a chain with v2 headers

Implements the server half of docs/electrum-header-v2.md, kept in one
place so replacing it with whatever upstream settles on means deleting a
section of headerv2.rs and the few call sites that name it, rather than
unpicking edits spread through electrum.rs.

The negotiated version becomes a property of the chain rather than a
constant. A chain that has produced a v2 header negotiates 1.8 and
refuses below it; every other chain negotiates 1.4 exactly as before.
Keyed on the tip actually being v2, not on the network name, so a chain
below its activation height keeps working with every existing client and
flips at the moment it crosses.

The refusal is the safety-critical half, not the number. A client below
1.8 does not fail loudly here: it reads 80 bytes of a 164-byte header,
gets something structurally plausible, computes SHA256d over it, and
cannot link the chain. The error says so, because a bare "unsupported
request" would send someone hunting the wrong fault.

server.features gains blake2b_fork, because genesis_hash cannot carry
chain identity on a fork that keeps its history: this chain and ordinary
testnet4 share a genesis block. Reported as a fork point rather than an
opaque id, since that needs no registry and a client can check it against
what the server then serves. The height is found by binary search over
the header chain rather than compiled in, so it is right on regtest where
the operator chose it.

Two items in the proposal need nothing here. Header length is already
read rather than assumed, because block.header and block.headers
serialize whatever the header is. Checkpoint proofs are not implemented
because electrs 0.11.1 has no cp_height at all; if that changes, leaves
at or above the activation height must be BLAKE2b, which
AnyHeader::block_hash already returns.

Verified against a regtest chain either side of its activation height.
Below it: Sparrow's range and Electrum's both negotiate 1.4, features
report 1.4 and no fork point. Above it: both are refused with the
explanation, a 1.8 client gets 1.8, and blake2b_fork reports height 20
with the right hash, found by search rather than told.
